ap:wrap;-webkit-box-align:center;-webkit-align-items:center;align-items:center;-webkit-box-pack:end;-webkit-justify-content:flex-end;justify-content:flex-end;-webkit-box-sizing:border-box;box-sizing:border-box;min-height:52px;margin:0;padding:0 24px var(--VcxYfc,20px);gap:8px;border-top:1px solid transparent}@media (forced-colors:active){.uW2Fw-T0kwCb{border-top-color:CanvasText}}.uW2Fw-Sx9Kwc-OWXEXe-eu7FSc .uW2Fw-T0kwCb{-webkit-box-orient:vertical;-webkit-box-direction:reverse;-webkit-flex-direction:column-reverse;flex-direction:column-reverse;-webkit-box-align:end;-webkit-align-items:flex-end;align-items:flex-end;gap:0}.uW2Fw-M1klYe:first-child{-webkit-margin-start:0;margin-inline-start:0}.uW2Fw-M1klYe{-webkit-margin-start:8px;margin-inline-start:8px;text-align:start;max-width:100%}.uW2Fw-Sx9Kwc-OWXEXe-eu7FSc .uW2Fw-M1klYe:not(:first-child){margin-top:12px}.uW2Fw-Sx9Kwc-OWXEXe-FNFY6c,.uW2Fw-Sx9Kwc-OWXEXe-FnSee,.uW2Fw-Sx9Kwc-OWXEXe-uGFO6d{display:-webkit-box;display:-webkit-flex;display:flex}.uW2Fw-Sx9Kwc-OWXEXe-uGFO6d .uW2Fw-IE5DDf{-webkit-transition:opacity .15s linear;transition:opacity .15s linear}.uW2Fw-Sx9Kwc-OWXEXe-uGFO6d .uW2Fw-wzTsW{-webkit-transition:opacity 75ms linear;transition:opacity 75ms linear}.uW2Fw-Sx9Kwc-OWXEXe-uGFO6d .uW2Fw-P5QLlc{-webkit-transition:-webkit-transform .15s cubic-bezier(0,0,.2,1);transition:-webkit-transform .15s cubic-bezier(0,0,.2,1);transition:transform .15s cubic-bezier(0,0,.2,1);transition:transform .15s cubic-bezier(0,0,.2,1),-webkit-transform .15s cubic-bezier(0,0,.2,1)}.uW2Fw-Sx9Kwc-OWXEXe-FnSee .uW2Fw-IE5DDf,.uW2Fw-Sx9Kwc-OWXEXe-FnSee .uW2Fw-wzTsW{-webkit-transition:opacity 75ms linear;transition:opacity 75ms linear}.uW2Fw-Sx9Kwc-OWXEXe-RTQbk .uW2Fw-IE5DDf{-webkit-transition:none;transition:none;opacity:1}.uW2Fw-Sx9Kwc-OWXEXe-FnSee .uW2Fw-P5QLlc{-webkit-transform:none;-ms-transform:none;transform:none}.uW2Fw-Sx9Kwc-OWXEXe-FNFY6c .uW2Fw-IE5DDf,.uW2Fw-Sx9Kwc-OWXEXe-FNFY6c .uW2Fw-wzTsW{opacity:1}.uW2Fw-Sx9Kwc-OWXEXe-FNFY6c .uW2Fw-P5QLlc{-webkit-transform:none;-ms-transform:none;transform:none}.uW2Fw-Sx9Kwc-XuHpsb-pGuBYc{overflow:hidden}.uW2Fw-IE5DDf-OWXEXe-L6cTce .uW2Fw-IE5DDf{opacity:0}.uW2Fw-IE5DDf-OWXEXe-uIDLbb{pointer-events:none}.uW2Fw-IE5DDf-OWXEXe-uIDLbb .uW2Fw-IE5DDf{display:none}.uW2Fw-Sx9Kwc:not(.uW2Fw-Sx9Kwc-OWXEXe-di8rgd-bN97Pc-QFlW2) .uW2Fw-cnG4Wd:last-child{-webkit-padding-after:var(--xkSh4b,var(--qqkfNd,24px));padding-block-end:var(--xkSh4b,var(--qqkfNd,24px))}.uW2Fw-IE5DDf-OWXEXe-uIDLbb .uW2Fw-P5QLlc{overflow:unset;--Fe5l0b:var(--gm3-sys-color-shadow,#000)}.uW2Fw-Sx9Kwc:not(.uW2Fw-Sx9Kwc-OWXEXe-eu7FSc) .uW2Fw-WP8JYd-LQLjdd{margin-right:auto}.uW2Fw-Sx9Kwc-OWXEXe-n2to0e .uW2Fw-k2Wrsb{color:var(--NAW3rf,var(--gm3-sys-color-on-surface,#1f1f1f));font-family:var(--Ux39Cb,\"Google Sans\");line\nThe above content was truncated because individual lines are very long. Only a portion of the content is shown.\n"}
{"step_index":8,"source":"MODEL","type":"GENERIC","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-08-24T02:18:37Z","content":"Created At: 2026-08-24T07:48:37+05:30\nCompleted At: 2026-08-24T07:48:38+05:30\nFile Path: `file:///C:/Users/renu5/.gemini/config/skills/prompt-forge/references/templates.md`\nTotal Lines: 453\nTotal Bytes: 17102\nShowing lines 1 to 453\nThe following code has been modified to include a line number before every line, in the format: <line_number>: <original_line>. Please note that any changes targeting the original code should remove the line number, colon, and leading space.\n1: # Prompt Templates Reference\r\n2: \r\n3: Full template library for Prompt Master. Read the relevant template when the user's task type matches. Do not load all templates at once — only the one you need.\r\n4: \r\n5: ## Table of Contents\r\n6: \r\n7: | Template | Best For |\r\n8: |----------|----------|\r\n9: | [A — RTF](#template-a--rtf) | Simple one-shot tasks |\r\n10: | [B — CO-STAR](#template-b--co-star) | Professional documents, business writing |\r\n11: | [C — RISEN](#template-c--risen) | Complex multi-step projects |\r\n12: | [D — CRISPE](#template-d--crispe) | Creative work, brand voice |\r\n13: | [E — Chain of Thought](#template-e--chain-of-thought) | Logic, math, analysis, debugging |\r\n14: | [F — Few-Shot](#template-f--few-shot) | Consistent structured output, pattern replication |\r\n15: | [G — File-Scope](#template-g--file-scope) | Cursor, Windsurf, Copilot — code editing AI |\r\n16: | [H — ReAct + Stop Conditions](#template-h--react--stop-conditions) | Claude Code, Devin — autonomous agents |\r\n17: | [I — Visual Descriptor](#template-i--visual-descriptor) | Midjourney, DALL-E, Stable Diffusion, Sora |\r\n18: | [J — Reference Image Editing](#template-j--reference-image-editing) | Editing an existing image with a reference |\r\n19: | [K — ComfyUI](#template-k--comfyui) | ComfyUI node-based image workflows |\r\n20: | [L — Prompt Decompiler](#template-l--prompt-decompiler) | Breaking down, adapting, or splitting existing prompts |\r\n21: | [M — Opus 4.7 / 4.8 Task Brief](#template-m--opus-4.7--4.8-task-brief) | Complex, multi-step, or agentic task on Claude Opus 4.7 or 4.8 |\r\n22: \r\n23: ---\r\n24: \r\n25: ## Template A — RTF\r\n26: \r\n27: *Role, Task, Format. Use for fast one-shot tasks where the request is clear and simple.*\r\n28: \r\n29: ```\r\n30: Role: [One sentence defining who the AI is]\r\n31: Task: [Precise verb + what to produce]\r\n32: Format: [Exact output format and length]\r\n33: ```\r\n34: \r\n35: **Example:**\r\n36: ```\r\n37: Role: You are a senior technical writer.\r\n38: Task: Write a one-paragraph description of what a REST API is.\r\n39: Format: Plain prose, 3 sentences maximum, no jargon, suitable for a non-technical audience.\r\n40: ```\r\n41: \r\n42: ---\r\n43: \r\n44: ## Template B — CO-STAR\r\n45: \r\n46: *Context, Objective, Style, Tone, Audience, Response. Use for professional documents, business writing, reports, and marketing content where full context control matters.*\r\n47: \r\n48: ```\r\n49: Context: [Background the AI needs to understand the situation]\r\n50: Objective: [Exact goal — what success looks like]\r\n51: Style: [Writing style: formal / conversational / technical / narrative]\r\n52: Tone: [Emotional register: authoritative / empathetic / urgent / neutral]\r\n53: Audience: [Who reads this — their knowledge level and expectations]\r\n54: Response: [Format, length, and structure of the output]\r\n55: ```\r\n56: \r\n57: **Example:**\r\n58: ```\r\n59: Context: I am a founder pitching a B2B SaaS tool that automates expense reporting for mid-size companies.\r\n60: Objective: Write a cold email that gets a reply from a CFO.\r\n61: Style: Direct and conversational, not salesy.\r\n62: Tone: Confident but not pushy.\r\n63: Audience: CFO at a 200-person company, busy, skeptical of vendor emails.\r\n64: Response: 5 sentences max. Subject line included. No bullet points.\r\n65: ```\r\n66: \r\n67: ---\r\n68: \r\n69: ## Template C — RISEN\r\n70: \r\n71: *Role, Instructions, Steps, End Goal, Narrowing. Use for complex projects, multi-step tasks, and any output that requires a clear sequence of actions.*\r\n72: \r\n73: ```\r\n74: Role: [Expert identity the AI should adopt]\r\n75: Instructions: [Overall task in plain terms]\r\n76: Steps:\r\n77:   1. [First action]\r\n78:   2. [Second action]\r\n79:   3. [Continue as needed]\r\n80: End Goal: [What the final output must achieve]\r\n81: Narrowing: [Constraints, scope limits, what to exclude]\r\n82: ```\r\n83: \r\n84: **Example:**\r\n85: ```\r\n86: Role: You are a product manager with 10 years of experience in mobile apps.\r\n87: Instructions: Write a product requirements document for a habit tracking feature.\r\n88: Steps:\r\n89:   1. Define the problem statement in one paragraph\r\n90:   2. List user stories in the format \"As a [user], I want [goal] so that [reason]\"\r\n91:   3. Define acceptance criteria for each story\r\n92:   4. List out-of-scope items explicitly\r\n93: End Goal: A PRD that an engineering team can begin sprint planning from immediately.\r\n94: Narrowing: No technical implementation details. No wireframes. Under 600 words total.\r\n95: ```\r\n96: \r\n97: ---\r\n98: \r\n99: ## Template D — CRISPE\r\n100: \r\n101: *Capacity, Role, Insight, Statement, Personality, Experiment. Use for creative work, brand voice writing, and any task where personality, tone, and iteration matter.*\r\n102: \r\n103: ```\r\n104: Capacity: [What capability or expertise the AI should have]\r\n105: Role: [Specific persona to adopt]\r\n106: Insight: [Key background insight that shapes the response]\r\n107: Statement: [The core task or question]\r\n108: Personality: [Tone and style — witty / authoritative / casual / sharp]\r\n109: Experiment: [Request variants or alternatives to explore]\r\n110: ```\r\n111: \r\n112: **Example:**\r\n113: ```\r\n114: Capacity: Expert copywriter specializing in SaaS product launches.\r\n115: Role: Brand voice for a productivity tool aimed at developers.\r\n116: Insight: Developers hate marketing speak and respond to honesty and specificity.\r\n117: Statement: Write the hero headline and sub-headline for the landing page.\r\n118: Personality: Sharp, dry, confident — no adjectives, no exclamation marks.\r\n119: Experiment: Give 3 variants ranging from minimal to bold.\r\n120: ```\r\n121: \r\n122: ---\r\n123: \r\n124: ## Template E — Chain of Thought\r\n125: \r\n126: *Use for logic-heavy tasks, math, debugging, and multi-factor analysis where the AI needs to reason carefully before committing to an answer.*\r\n127: \r\n128: **Important:** Only use CoT for standard reasoning models (Claude, GPT-4o, Gemini). Do NOT add CoT instructions to o1, o3, or Claude extended thinking — they reason internally and CoT instructions degrade their output.\r\n129: \r\n130: ```\r\n131: [Task statement]\r\n132: \r\n133: Before answering, think through this carefully:\r\n134: <thinking>\r\n135: 1. What is the actual problem being asked?\r\n136: 2. What constraints must the solution respect?\r\n137: 3. What are the possible approaches?\r\n138: 4. Which approach is best and why?\r\n139: </thinking>\r\n140: \r\n141: Give your final answer in <answer> tags only.\r\n142: ```\r\n143: \r\n144: **When to use:**\r\n145: - Debugging where the cause is not obvious\r\n146: - Comparing two technical approaches\r\n147: - Any math or calculation\r\n148: - Analysis where a wrong first impression is likely\r\n149: \r\n150: **When NOT to use:**\r\n151: - o1 / o3 / reasoning models (they think internally — adding CoT hurts)\r\n152: - Simple tasks where the answer is clear (unnecessary overhead)\r\n153: - Creative tasks (CoT can kill natural voice)\r\n154: \r\n155: ---\r\n156: \r\n157: ## Template F — Few-Shot\r\n158: \r\n159: *Use when the output format is easier to show than describe. Examples outperform written instructions for format-sensitive tasks every time.*\r\n160: \r\n161: ```\r\n162: [Task instruction]\r\n163: \r\n164: Here are examples of the exact format needed:\r\n165: \r\n166: <examples>\r\n167:   <example>\r\n168:     <input>[example input 1]</input>\r\n169:     <output>[example output 1]</output>\r\n170:   </example>\r\n171:   <example>\r\n172:     <input>[example input 2]</input>\r\n173:     <output>[example output 2]</output>\r\n174:   </example>\r\n175: </examples>\r\n176: \r\n177: Now apply this exact pattern to: [actual input]\r\n178: ```\r\n179: \r\n180: **Rules:**\r\n181: - 2 to 5 examples is the sweet spot. More rarely helps and wastes tokens.\r\n182: - Examples must include edge cases, not just easy cases.\r\n183: - Use XML tags to wrap examples — Claude parses XML reliably.\r\n184: - If you have been re-prompting for the same formatting correction twice, switch to few-shot instead of rewriting instructions.\r\n185: \r\n186: ---\r\n187: \r\n188: ## Template G — File-Scope\r\n189: \r\n190: *Use for Cursor, Windsurf, GitHub Copilot, and any AI that edits code inside a codebase. The most common failure mode here is editing the wrong file or breaking existing logic — this template prevents both.*\r\n191: \r\n192: ```\r\n193: File: [exact/path/to/file.ext]\r\n194: Function/Component: [exact name]\r\n195: \r\n196: Current Behavior:\r\n197: [What this code does right now — be specific]\r\n198: \r\n199: Desired Change:\r\n200: [What it should do after the edit — be specific]\r\n201: \r\n202: Scope:\r\n203: Only modify [function / component / section].\r\n204: Do NOT touch: [list everything to leave unchanged]\r\n205: \r\n206: Constraints:\r\n207: - Language/framework: [specify version]\r\n208: - Do not add dependencies not in [package.json / requirements.txt]\r\n209: - Preserve existing [type signatures / API contracts / variable names]\r\n210: \r\n211: Done When:\r\n212: [Exact condition that confirms the change worked correctly]\r\n213: ```\r\n214: \r\n215: ---\r\n216: \r\n217: ## Template H — ReAct + Stop Conditions\r\n218: \r\n219: *Use for Claude Code, Devin, AutoGPT, and any AI that takes autonomous actions. Runaway loops and scope explosion are the biggest credit killers in agentic workflows — stop conditions are not optional.*\r\n220: \r\n221: ```\r\n222: Objective:\r\n223: [Single, unambiguous goal in one sentence]\r\n224: \r\n225: Starting State:\r\n226: [Current file structure / codebase state / environment]\r\n227: \r\n228: Target State:\r\n229: [What should exist when the agent is done]\r\n230: \r\n231: Allowed Actions:\r\n232: - [Specific action the agent may take]\r\n233: - Install only packages listed in [requirements.txt / package.json]\r\n234: \r\n235: Forbidden Actions:\r\n236: - Do NOT modify files outside [directory/scope]\r\n237: - Do NOT run the dev server or deploy\r\n238: - Do NOT push to git\r\n239: - Do NOT delete files without showing a diff first\r\n240: - Do NOT make architecture decisions without human approval\r\n241: \r\n242: Stop Conditions:\r\n243: Pause and ask for human review when:\r\n244: - A file would be permanently deleted\r\n245: - A new external service or API needs to be integrated\r\n246: - Two valid implementation paths exist and the choice affects architecture\r\n247: - An error cannot be resolved in 2 attempts\r\n248: - The task requires changes outside the stated scope\r\n249: \r\n250: Checkpoints:\r\n251: After each major step, output: ✅ [what was completed]\r\n252: At the end, output a full summary of every file changed.\r\n253: ```\r\n254: \r\n255: ---\r\n256: \r\n257: ## Template I — Visual Descriptor\r\n258: \r\n259: *Use for Midjourney, DALL-E 3, Stable Diffusion, Sora, Runway, and any image or video generation tool.*\r\n260: \r\n261: ```\r\n262: Subject: [Main subject — specific, not vague]\r\n263: Action/Pose: [What the subject is doing]\r\n264: Setting: [Where the scene takes place]\r\n265: Style: [photorealistic / cinematic / anime / oil painting / vector / etc.]\r\n266: Mood: [dramatic / serene / eerie / joyful / etc.]\r\n267: Lighting: [golden hour / studio / neon / overcast / candlelight / etc.]\r\n268: Color Palette: [dominant colors or named palette]\r\n269: Composition: [wide shot / close-up / aerial / Dutch angle / etc.]\r\n270: Aspect Ratio: [16:9 / 1:1 / 9:16 / 4:3]\r\n271: Negative Prompts: [blurry, watermark, extra fingers, distortion, low quality]\r\n272: Style Reference: [artist / film / aesthetic reference if applicable]\r\n273: ```\r\n274: \r\n275: **Tool-specific syntax:**\r\n276: - **Midjourney**: Comma-separated descriptors, not prose. Add `--ar`, `--style`, `--v 6` at the end.\r\n277: - **Stable Diffusion**: Use `(word:1.3)` weight syntax. CFG scale 7 to 12. Negative prompt is mandatory.\r\n278: - **DALL-E 3**: Prose works well. Add \"do not include any text in the image\" unless text is needed.\r\n279: - **Sora / video**: Add camera movement (slow dolly, static shot, crane up), duration in seconds, and cut style.\r\n280: \r\n281: ---\r\n282: \r\n283: ## Template J — Reference Image Editing\r\n284: \r\n285: *Use when the user has an existing image they want to modify. Completely different from generation — never describe the whole scene from scratch, only describe the change.*\r\n286: \r\n287: **Before writing the prompt, always tell the user:**\r\n288: \"Attach your reference image to [tool name] before sending this prompt.\"\r\n289: \r\n290: **Detect the tool's editing capability:**\r\n291: - Midjourney: use `--cref [image URL]` for character reference or `--sref` for style reference\r\n292: - DALL-E 3: use the Edit endpoint, not the Generate endpoint. User must be in ChatGPT with image editing enabled\r\n293: - Stable Diffusion: use img2img mode, not txt2img. Set denoising strength 0.3-0.6 to preserve the original\r\n294: \r\n295: ```\r\n296: Reference image: [attached / URL]\r\n297: What to keep exactly the same: [list everything that must not change]\r\n298: What to change: [specific edit only — be precise]\r\n299: How much to change: [subtle / moderate / significant]\r\n300: Style consistency: maintain the exact style, lighting, and mood of the reference\r\n301: Negative prompt: [what to avoid introducing]\r\n302: ```\r\n303: \r\n304: **Example:**\r\n305: ```\r\n306: Reference image: [attached portrait photo]\r\n307: What to keep exactly the same: face, hair, clothing, background, lighting\r\n308: What to change: head angle — rotate from facing left to facing straight forward\r\n309: How much to change: subtle, preserve all facial features exactly\r\n310: Style consistency: maintain photorealistic style, same lighting direction\r\n311: Negative prompt: no new elements, no style changes, no background changes\r\n312: ```\r\n313: \r\n314: ---\r\n315: \r\n316: ## Template K — ComfyUI\r\n317: \r\n318: *Use for ComfyUI node-based workflows. Always output Positive and Negative prompts as separate blocks. Ask for the checkpoint model before writing — syntax and token limits differ per model.*\r\n319: \r\n320: **Ask first if not stated:**\r\n321: \"Which checkpoint model are you using? (SD 1.5, SDXL, Flux, or other)\"\r\n322: \r\n323: **Model-specific notes:**\r\n324: - SD 1.5: shorter prompts work better, under 75 tokens per block, use (word:weight) syntax\r\n325: - SDXL: handles longer prompts, supports more natural language alongside weighted syntax\r\n326: - Flux: natural language works well, less reliance on weighted syntax, very responsive to style descriptions\r\n327: \r\n328: ```\r\n329: POSITIVE PROMPT:\r\n330: [subject], [style], [mood], [lighting], [composition], [quality boosters: highly detailed, sharp focus, 8k]\r\n331: \r\n332: NEGATIVE PROMPT:\r\n333: [what to exclude: blurry, low quality, watermark, extra limbs, bad anatomy, distorted, oversaturated]\r\n334: \r\n335: CHECKPOINT: [model name]\r\n336: SAMPLER: Euler a (recommended starting point)\r\n337: CFG SCALE: 7 (increase for stricter prompt adherence)\r\n338: STEPS: 20-30\r\n339: RESOLUTION: [width x height — must be divisible by 64]\r\n340: ```\r\n341: \r\n342: ---\r\n343: \r\n344: ## Template L — Prompt Decompiler\r\n345: \r\n346: *Use when the user pastes an existing prompt and wants to break it down, adapt it for a different tool, simplify it, or understand its structure. This is analysis and adaptation, not building from scratch.*\r\n347: \r\n348: **Detect which Decompiler task is needed:**\r\n349: - **Break down** — explain what each part of the prompt does\r\n350: - **Adapt** — rewrite for a different tool while preserving intent\r\n351: - **Simplify** — remove redundancy and tighten without losing meaning\r\n352: - **Split** — divide a complex one-shot prompt into a cleaner sequence\r\n353: \r\n354: **For Adapt tasks, always ask:**\r\n355: \"What tool is the original prompt from, and what tool are you adapting it for?\"\r\n356: \r\n357: **Break down output format:**\r\n358: ```\r\n359: Original prompt: [paste]\r\n360: \r\n361: Structure analysis:\r\n362: - Role/Identity: [what role is assigned and why]\r\n363: - Task: [what action is being requested]\r\n364: - Constraints: [what limits are set]\r\n365: - Format: [what output shape is expected]\r\n366: - Weaknesses: [what is missing or could cause wrong output]\r\n367: \r\n368: Recommended fix: [rewritten version with gaps filled]\r\n369: ```\r\n370: \r\n371: **Adapt output format:**\r\n372: ```\r\n373: Original ([source tool]): [original prompt]\r\n374: \r\n375: Adapted for [target tool]:\r\n376: [rewritten prompt using target tool syntax and best practices]\r\n377: \r\n378: Key changes made:\r\n379: - [change 1 and why]\r\n380: - [change 2 and why]\r\n381: ```\r\n382: \r\n383: **Split output format:**\r\n384: ```\r\n385: Original prompt: [paste]\r\n386: \r\n387: This prompt is doing [N] things. Split into [N] sequential prompts:\r\n388: \r\n389: Prompt 1 — [what it handles]:\r\n390: [prompt block]\r\n391: \r\n392: Prompt 2 — [what it handles]:\r\n393: [prompt block]\r\n394: \r\n395: Run these in order. Each output feeds the next.\r\n396: ```\r\n397: ---\r\n398: \r\n399: ## Template M — Opus 4.7 / 4.8 Task Brief\r\n400: \r\n401: *Use for any complex, multi-step, or agentic task on Claude Opus 4.7 or 4.8 (current default) — claude.ai, API, or Claude Code. Both read prompts literally and produce narrow output when context is missing. This template front-loads everything so the first turn is the only turn.*\r\n402: \r\n403: ```\r\n404: ## Objective\r\n405: [What needs to be built, fixed, or produced — one clear sentence. Add WHY if it affects approach.]\r\n406: \r\n407: ## Context\r\n408: [What exists now — relevant files, current behavior, stack already in place, what was tried and failed]\r\n409: \r\n410: ## Target State\r\n411: [What done looks like — specific files changed, behavior produced, tests passing. Binary where possible.]\r\n412: \r\n413: ## Scope\r\n414: - Work only in: [specific files and directories]\r\n415: - Do NOT touch: [forbidden files — .env, package-lock.json, configs, anything outside scope]\r\n416: \r\n417: ## Constraints\r\n418: - [Stack version, naming conventions, no new dependencies without asking]\r\n419: - Only make changes directly requested. Do not add features, abstractions, or files beyond what was asked.\r\n420: \r\n421: ## Acceptance Criteria\r\n422: - [ ] [Binary check 1]\r\n423: - [ ] [Binary check 2]\r\n424: - [ ] [Binary check 3]\r\n425: \r\n426: ## Stop Conditions\r\n427: Stop and ask before:\r\n428: - Deleting any file\r\n429: - Adding any dependency\r\n430: - Modifying database schema or migrations\r\n431: - Touching anything outside Scope\r\n432: \r\n433: ## Progress\r\n434: After each completed step: ✅ [what was done] — [file(s) affected]\r\n435: ```\r\n436: \r\n437: **Thinking depth** — add only when needed, delete otherwise:\r\n438: - Hard multi-step task: `\"Think carefully and step-by-step before starting.\"`\r\n439: - Simple targeted change: `\"Prioritize responding quickly. This is a scoped change.\"`\r\n440: - Default: say nothing — adaptive thinking calibrates itself.\r\n441: \r\n442: **Claude Code only — add Session Strategy block when relevant:**\r\n443: ```\r\n444: ## Session Strategy\r\n445: [Pick one:]\r\n446: - New session — unrelated to prior context, start fresh\r\n447: - Continue — prior context still needed\r\n448: - Subagent — spin off for [file-heavy research / verification] so intermediate output stays out of main context\r\n449: - Compact first — run /compact [focus on X] then begin\r\n450: ```\r\n451: \r\n452: **When to use:** Opus 4.7 or 4.8 on any surface — claude.ai, API, Claude Code — when the task is complex, multi-file, ambiguous, or agentic. Not needed for simple one-shot tasks.\r\n453: \nThe above content shows the entire, complete file contents of the requested file.\n"}
